 Wright","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10765","person_slug":"penny-wright","vote":"no"},{"name":"Peter Whish-Wilson","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10780","person_slug":"peter-whish-wilson","vote":"no"},{"name":"Rachel Siewert","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10584","person_slug":"rachel-siewert","vote":"no"},{"name":"Richard Colbeck","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10131","person_slug":"richard-colbeck","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Richard Di Natale","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10755","person_slug":"richard-di-natale","vote":"no"},{"name":"Sam Dastyari","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10819","person_slug":"sam-dastyari","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Sarah Hanson-Young","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10711","person_slug":"sarah-hanson-young","vote":"no"},{"name":"Scott Ludlam","party":"Greens","person_id":"tvfy_10713","person_slug":"scott-ludlam","vote":"no"},{"name":"Sean Edwards","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10756","person_slug":"sean-edwards","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Stephen Parry","party":"PRES","person_id":"tvfy_10507","person_slug":"stephen-parry","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Sue Boyce","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10062","person_slug":"sue-boyce","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Sue Lines","party":"PRES","person_id":"tvfy_10782","person_slug":"sue-lines","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Ursula Stephens","party":"Labor","person_id":"tvfy_10609","person_slug":"ursula-stephens","vote":"aye"},{"name":"Zed Seselja","party":"Liberal","person_id":"tvfy_10822","person_slug":"zed-seselja","vote":"aye"}],"name":"Migration Amendment Bill 2013 - Second Reading - Read a second time","noes":9,"question":"The majority voted in favour of a [motion](http://www.openaustralia.org/senate/?gid=2014-05-14.13.18) to read the bill for a second time.(Read more about the stages that a bill must pass through to become law [here](http://www.peo.gov.au/learning/fact-sheets/making-a-law.html). ) This means that the majority agree with the main idea of the bill and that the Senate can now consider it in more detail. Alternatively, the Senate can vote on whether to read the bill for a third time without considering it in any more detail.(In the case of this bill, the Senate did decide to immediately vote on whether to read the bill for a third time without considering it further (see that division [here](http://publicwhip-rails.openaustraliafoundation.org.au/division.php?date=2014-05-14&number=3&house=senate)). ) _Background to the bill_ The bill was introduced in response to recent court and tribunal decisions. Their purpose is to: - resolve questions about the day and time at which certain decisions of the Minister (or their delegate) are taken to be finally made and at which decisions of the [Migration Review Tribunal](http://www.mrt-rrt.gov.au/About-Us.aspx) (MRT) and [Refugee Review Tribunal](http://www.mrt-rrt.gov.au/About-Us.aspx) (RRT) are made and become final; - ensure that, once an application for a protection visa has been refused, or a protection visa has been cancelled, a person cannot apply for a protection visa on any other ground while in the [migration zone](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_migration_zone); - make it a criterion for the grant of a protection visa in [section 36](http://www.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/ma1958118/s36.html) of the [Migration Act](http://www.austlii.edu.au/au/legis/cth/consol_act/ma1958118/) that the applicant is not assessed by the [Australian Security Intelligence Organisation](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_Security_Intelligence_Organisation) to be directly or indirectly a risk to security within the meaning","result":"affirmative","slug":"division-federal-senate-3199","source_url":"https://theyvoteforyou.org.au/divisions/senate/2014-05-14/2"}
